WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Lab diamonds now cost $11 a carrot to grow, against $93 for mind-ruff. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 164. That growing cost is a measured average, not a brochure price. The Edon Golden Diamond Research Report for 2025 put gem-quality laboratory stones at $10.85 a carrot, across $69.4 million carrots made in a year. The carbon in them is not what you are paying for.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.380
The bill is the machine that arranges it. Tracey Hall walked into the General Electric

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:41.040
Lab on December 16, 1954, and ran graphite mixed with iron sulfide through a press he

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.680
had redesigned himself. After 38 minutes at roughly 100,000 atmospheres and 1600 degrees

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.860
Celsius, he opened the chamber. Inside were octahedral crystals that scratched glass and passed

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.560
every test for diamond. That single run ended a century of failed claims.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.220
Carbon could be forced into the right lattice without anyone digging it out of the ground.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.720
Hall had stripped the problem to the physical requirement. Carbon has to reach the pressure

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.960
and temperature region where diamond is the stable phase. Earlier massive presses had cracked

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:23.020
under the load, so he discarded them. He machined a belt apparatus whose tapered pistons and shrink

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.800
reaction volume. The iron sulfide catalyst lowered the temperature needed for

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.560
re-crystallization. The belt held long enough for crystals to nucleate and grow

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.880
to roughly one millimeter. A nature paper from the general electric team

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.560
recorded yields from dust smaller than 100 microns up to quarter-carot stones

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:47.160
and confirmed identity by x-ray diffraction, density, and hardness. Within months

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.740
the same team scaled the process. For most of the 20th century the trade had

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:57.280
assumed the only reliable source was an ancient volcanic pipe, mined at great depth and expense.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.080
Miners extracted Kimberlight ore, crushed it, and recovered sparse crystals.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.680
The price of that rough reflected shafts, labor, sorting, and the control of a handful

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.620
of large producers.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.020
Every step after extraction added sorting, certification, and distribution that had

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:14.940
never been questioned.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.000
No one asked whether the crystal lattice itself required those steps.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.280
The industry reproduced the same extraction model year after year, so the finished price

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.920
was set by geology and logistics rather than by the cost of arranging carbon atoms.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:34.940
Early laboratory attempts failed to match natural hardness or size, and the failures reinforced

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.620
the belief that only nature could deliver the real thing.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.220
Investment in synthesis stayed small.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.920
The price of any diamond stayed far above the cost of its atoms.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.380
One objection said laboratory material would always lack the optical or mechanical properties

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.460
of mine stones.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.960
Repeated tests on the first general electric crystals showed identical density, refractive

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.820
index, and thermal conductivity, once the lattice formed correctly.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.600
Another objection said scale would stay impossible unless someone replicated the slow geological

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:06.120
timeline.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.860
The controlled conditions inside the press and later inside a reactor compress that timeline

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.720
into hours rather than millennia.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.380
Decades after Hall, workers at NIRM in Japan took the lattice off the high pressure belt.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:25.040
They showed that methane diluted in hydrogen could deposit diamond at low pressure, once

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.940
atomic hydrogen etched away the graphitic carbon that wanted to form instead.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.800
Growth rates moved from fractions of a micron per hour to several microns per hour as gas

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.220
mixtures and plasma activation improved.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:41.340
Each gain in rate and crystal size cut the reactor time per carrot.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:48.060
Chemical vapor deposition, CVD, could grow gem-quality stones from methane at far lower pressures

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:48.800
than the belt.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.980
The variable was still the lattice.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.660
The machine had changed.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.940
Here is the floor, wave a magic wand, and arrange the raw atoms into the finished crystal

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:02.740
for free, and the only bill left is the carbon, supplied as graphite or as methane.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:08.700
That magic wand number sits well below $1 a carrot, even before you account for the small

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.620
fraction of carbon that actually incorporates into the lattice.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:17.280
The documented growing cost is the $10.85 from the Edon Golden Report.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.700
Divide that growing cost by a floor under $1.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.700
If the carbon cost a full dollar, the quotient would already sit a little above $10.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.880
A cheaper floor pushes it higher.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.880
The first principal's ratio is still above $10.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.660
It has also fallen by more than tenfold from the days when every diamond required a

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:35.960
mine.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.980
The ratio is a location, not a verdict on the people who built the mines or the reactors.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.500
What it shows is where the gap sits.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.880
The remaining premium lives in reactor electricity, substrate preparation, post-growth annealing,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.680
and the cutting and polishing that turned rough crystal into a faceted gem.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.220
A frequent question is whether the electricity alone could erase the advantage.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:03.480
In practice, the energy cost per carrot has dropped in step with faster growth rates

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.920
and larger batch sizes.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:10.280
That has kept the fabrication premium modest next to the old mining and sorting overhead.

00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:13.613
The stones themselves have moved with the process.

00:05:14.333 --> 00:05:19.093
Laboratory material has gone from sub-carrot industrial grit to majority production above

00:05:19.093 --> 00:05:23.733
three carrots, with color and clarity that compete directly with many mined gems.

00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:23.913
programs.

00:05:24.413 --> 00:05:30.373
Production volumes reported by the Gemological Institute of America, GIA, show CVD now

00:05:30.373 --> 00:05:35.913
dominates submissions, and those stones routinely receive a post-growth high-pressure, high-temperature

00:05:35.913 --> 00:05:37.933
treatment to remove brown coloration.

00:05:38.653 --> 00:05:43.993
Laboratory Ruff now trades near $16 a carrot, roughly $1.6 the natural price.

00:05:44.653 --> 00:05:49.013
Substitution has begun in the segments where buyers accept the laboratory product once

00:05:49.013 --> 00:05:51.233
certification confirms the property's match.

00:05:51.233 --> 00:05:57.133
Limits are still visible, a run needs seed crystals, plasma or high pressure still

00:05:57.133 --> 00:06:02.993
cost energy to maintain. Truly colorless type 2A material is hard to grow without additional

00:06:02.993 --> 00:06:08.553
processing. Those constraints keep the first principles ratio above one. They are why

00:06:08.553 --> 00:06:13.133
the cheapest laboratory diamond still carry a measurable fabrication premium, instead of

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:17.953
selling at the price of bulk carbon. One remaining objection is that demand for mine

00:06:17.953 --> 00:06:21.753
stones will hold their price no matter how much laboratory supply arrives.

00:06:22.333 --> 00:06:26.793
The trading gap already on the table says that objection does not hold everywhere buyers

00:06:26.793 --> 00:06:27.913
can check the certificate.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:30.859
The portable piece is the redefinition.

00:06:31.519 --> 00:06:36.159
A diamond is an arrangement of carbon atoms, not a mineral that must be located in a pipe.

00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:39.899
Hall's pressure temperature map once looked like a reason to dig.

00:06:40.359 --> 00:06:46.839
The same map now fits inside a reusable reactor, a belt or a CVD chamber whose yield improves

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:50.039
with every refinement of gas chemistry or belt geometry.

00:06:50.679 --> 00:06:53.099
Once the lattice is grown rather than excavated,

00:06:53.219 --> 00:06:54.999
the invoice stops describing the hole

00:06:54.999 --> 00:06:56.699
and starts describing the process.

00:06:57.319 --> 00:06:58.819
That is what the ratio is for.

00:06:59.359 --> 00:07:01.619
It applies to a thing that is designed and made,

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:04.219
a part, a product, a plant, a process,

00:07:04.439 --> 00:07:06.359
a service built from repeatable steps.

00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:08.959
A large ratio means most of the price

00:07:08.959 --> 00:07:11.059
is designed, process, organization,

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:12.959
or rules rather than material.

00:07:13.439 --> 00:07:15.379
And that is where a from scratch rethink

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:20.339
has room to work. It says nothing useful about a price set by scarcity of location,

00:07:20.899 --> 00:07:26.459
a captive audience, or a brand. When a gap turns out to be one of those, say so, and move on.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:32.019
Do not dress a market power story as an engineering one. In this case, the properties matched,

00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:37.799
the timeline collapsed from millennia to hours, and the energy costs per carat fell as the rate rose.

00:07:38.359 --> 00:07:42.839
The premium that moved was processed. The premium that remains is still processed.

00:07:42.839 --> 00:07:46.139
this, electricity, the seed, the anneal, the polish.

00:07:46.619 --> 00:07:48.819
Those steps can be timed and redesigned.

00:07:49.239 --> 00:07:51.659
They are also why the ratio has not reached one.

00:07:52.239 --> 00:07:56.559
The same accounting is open to any everyday material whose price still reflects the whole

00:07:56.559 --> 00:07:59.279
it came out of, rather than the atoms it contains.

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:04.359
If the required ingredient is common, and the expensive part is an inherited route for

00:08:04.359 --> 00:08:09.499
arranging it, the magic wand number is the atoms and everything above that floor is a candidate.

00:08:09.499 --> 00:08:15.399
it. Not every candidate falls. Seeds, energy, and finishing can hold a ratio above one for

00:08:15.399 --> 00:08:20.479
a long time. The test is whether those costs shrink when the rate improves, the way the

00:08:20.479 --> 00:08:25.679
energy per carat did when growth sped up and batches got larger. Tomorrow the show returns

00:08:25.679 --> 00:08:30.059
with another concrete case or another industry whose numbers invite the same accounting.

00:08:30.619 --> 00:08:34.979
Which other everyday material still carries a price set by the hole it came out of, rather

00:08:34.979 --> 00:08:40.599
than the atoms it contains. That's First Principles Daily for today. If this changed how you see

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:45.359
what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated, and subscribe wherever you listen.

